nap/new/ARC1_code/train/d406998b.py:32:    assert fg_colors == [Color.GREY], \"expected grey as the only foreground color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/72ca375d.py:20:    - One sprite is symmetric under a rotation, reflection, or diagonal symmetry.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/b0f4d537.py:100:    - The grid has black background with grey separator/filler cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/08ed6ac7.py:10:# In the input you will see a row of exactly 4 grey bars of different heights, each starting at the bottom of the canvas, and each separated by 1 pixel (so they are two pixels apart)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6855a6e4.py:60:    # Legacy note: the seed solver ran under a transpose wrapper, so the axis test swaps here.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6855a6e4.py:90:        # Conjugation note: legacy seeds ran under a transpose wrapper.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/3618c87e.py:67:    # bottom baseline row is grey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/3618c87e.py:70:    # randomly select 1 or 2 columns to have a blue pixel above a grey stack\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e40b9e2f.py:81:    - For each foreground pixel in the inferred square, paint its positions under 0, 90, 180, and 270 degree rotations about the square center.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:13:# In the input, you should see a black grid with nine 3x3 grey squares randomly placed in it (some of the squares touch a little bit). Each square contains a colored object of a different color, 3-4 cells in area, except for one which is blank. The colored objects are at the border of the 3x3 shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:14:# To make the output, create a 9x9 grey grid. Now place each of the 3x3 squares from the input grid into the output grid. The location of an object is done so that the colored object in the grey square is moved \"away\" fromm the center square of the output grid in the direction the colored object is in the 3x3 square.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:34:    # 1. Extract the 3x3 grey squares from the input grid (tricky because sometimes they touch, so we can't use connected components; detect_objects works better)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:36:    # 3. Place the 3x3 squares into the output grid by sliding it in the direction of the colored (non-grey) portion\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:38:    # step 1: extract the 3x3 squares, which are grey+another color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:53:    assert len(square_sprites) == 9, \"There should be exactly 9 3x3 grey squares in the input grid\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:55:    # step 2: create the output grid, which is all grey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:61:    # we can ignore the blank square, since the middle is already grey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:89:        grey_center_of_mass = np.argwhere(square == Color.GREY).mean(axis=0)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:91:        dr, dc = get_direction_between(grey_center_of_mass, colored_object_center_of_mass)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:108:    # 1. create nine 3x3 grey squares with colored objects in them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a8c38be5.py:115:            # Middle square is all grey (blank square)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/d2abd087.py:9:# The input consists of several grey objects in a 10x10 grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:10:    Use the grey pixels as a mask for the only other input color and turn everything else black.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:19:    - Recolor every grey input pixel to the unique non-grey input color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:20:    - Recolor every non-grey input pixel to black.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:31:    assert Color.GREY in colors, \"expected grey mask pixels\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:43:# Canvas, palette, grey density, and retry limits.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:54:    min_grey = max(1, int(np.ceil(GREY_RATIO_RANGE[0] * total)))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:55:    max_grey = min(total - 1, int(np.floor(GREY_RATIO_RANGE[1] * total)))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:56:    grey_count = int(np.random.randint(min_grey, max_grey + 1))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f76d97a5.py:59:    cells[:grey_count] = Color.GREY\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:11:# The input consists of a black grid containing a few hollow grey rectangles. Each rectangle has 1-3 grey horizontal or vertical lines emanating off of it (at most one per side), either travelling to the border or stopping at another rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:12:# To create the output, fill in the hollow grey rectangles with yellow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:32:    # extract objects using grey as background\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:49:            # to do so, check for grey pixels around the border of the grey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:92:    # add 2-4 grey rectangles with hollow insides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e73095fd.py:129:            # to check, look for grey pixel within 1 pixel of the rectangle\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/f560132c.py:46:    Unique orientations under rotations + mirror (D4).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:25:    Recolor the object whose shape matches the colored template inside the grey L-frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:28:    - The black background contains one grey L-frame, with its vertical leg on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:35:    - Extract the normalized mask of the colored template inside the grey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:39:    - Recolor that matched outside object to grey and leave all other pixels\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:43:    The grey L-frame is a shape selector: it marks the template to search for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:49:    assert bg != frame_color, \"grey is the frame color, not the background\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/63613498.py:53:    assert len(frames) == 1, \"expected exactly one grey L-frame\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/ac605cbb.py:39:    - The grey and yellow colors are absent from the input and are used only in the output trail drawing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/ac605cbb.py:42:    - Blue paints two grey pixels to the right and places a blue endpoint one row above the second grey pixel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/ac605cbb.py:43:    - Red paints three grey pixels to the left and places a red endpoint four columns left.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
